menu "KobitoKey Haptic"

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    bool "KobitoKey haptic motor support"
    default y
    depends on DT_HAS_KOBITOKEY_DRV2605L_ENABLED
    select I2C
    help
      The motor now lives behind a DRV2605L on i2c1, so this is only
      buildable where that node exists. Guarding it on the node keeps the
      shieldless builds -- settings_reset among them -- compiling.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_COOLDOWN_MS
    int "Shortest gap between ticks, in milliseconds"
    default 50
    range 0 2000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Applies either way the spacing is driven -- see
      KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE.

      By time: the gap the ticks close up to when the ball is being
      turned at COOLDOWN_FAST_SPEED or above. Also the gap used at every
      speed if COOLDOWN_SLOW_MS is not above it, which is how this
      behaved before the spacing was made to vary.

      By distance: a floor on how fast the motor can be asked to fire
      again, nothing more. Distance decides *whether* a tick was earned;
      this only limits how often the request can be honoured, which is
      a property of the motor rather than of the ball.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE
    bool "Space ticks by distance turned rather than by time"
    default n
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Until now the gap between ticks has been computed from how fast
      the ball is turning -- see KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_COOLDOWN_SLOW_MS and
      neighbours. This instead fires a tick every fixed step of
      movement (KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_NOTCH counts) and lets the timing
      fall out of that for free: turn twice as fast, and the same steps
      arrive twice as often. No speed is measured, smoothed, or curved
      to get there.

      The feel is not just a different mechanism, it is a different
      shape. Closing a *time* gap along a straight line makes the
      *rate* -- its reciprocal -- climb increasingly steeply, which is
      what gave the time-based version its sense of building up. Distance
      gives up that climb for exact proportionality: twice the speed is
      exactly twice the rate, the way spinning a real detented dial
      faster does not exaggerate into a crescendo, it just clicks more
      often.

      It also drops the floor. The time-based version keeps ticking at
      COOLDOWN_SLOW_MS even when the ball is barely turning, because a
      duration has to be picked regardless of speed. Distance has
      nothing to say about a ball that has not covered a step yet, so
      barely-there movement goes quiet rather than ticking on a timer.

      Position is tracked signed, so working back across a step before
      finishing it withdraws the credit rather than adding to it --
      rocking the ball across a boundary it has not yet crossed does not
      tick, the way it would if distance travelled were tracked instead
      of position reached.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_NOTCH
    int "Movement per tick, in common units"
    default 15
    range 1 65535
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE
    help
      This is the number to compare when tuning feel by hand, and it is
      meant to read the same in both .conf files -- see
      KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_SCALE for why it can.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_SCALE
    int "Raw counts per common unit, for this half"
    default 1
    range 1 65535
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE
    help
      The two halves' ticks do not count the same thing. The right
      half's sits after the vertical scroll scaler (1/8), so it counts
      scroll output; the left half's fires from a local input callback
      ahead of any scaling, so it counts the raw sensor. The same hand
      movement reads about twelve times higher on the left.

      Rather than let that show up as two unrelated numbers in
      TICK_NOTCH, it is factored out here: the threshold actually used
      is TICK_NOTCH x TICK_SCALE. Set TICK_NOTCH the same on both
      halves and let this carry the difference -- 1 on the right, 12 on
      the left. Whether the two are actually equal by feel is a
      separate question this cannot answer; it only keeps the numbers
      that describe them comparable.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_COOLDOWN_SLOW_MS
    int "Longest gap between ticks, in milliseconds"
    default 120
    range 0 2000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC && !KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE
    help
      The gap at COOLDOWN_SLOW_SPEED and below, and the gap a scroll
      starts from after a pause.

      Between the two speeds the gap closes towards COOLDOWN_MS, so the
      ticks arrive closer together the faster the ball turns. The gap is
      interpolated, not the rate, which means the rate itself accelerates:
      halfway up the speed range the gap is halfway between the two, but
      the ticks per second are not -- they are still nearer the slow end,
      and most of the rise lands in the upper part of the range.

      Set this at or below COOLDOWN_MS to go back to a fixed spacing.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_COOLDOWN_SLOW_SPEED
    int "Speed at or below which the ticks stay at their widest"
    default 60
    range 1 65535
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC && !KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE
    help
      In whatever counts per second reach the tick, which is not the same
      thing on the two halves.

      The right half's ticks are driven from an input processor that sits
      after the scroll scalers, so it counts scroll output -- roughly the
      ball's counts divided by the scaler's divisor. The left half's are
      driven from an input callback on the sensor itself, so it counts
      raw sensor counts. The left's numbers are therefore around a dozen
      times the right's for the same movement of the hand, and the two
      halves' values here are not meant to match.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_COOLDOWN_FAST_SPEED
    int "Speed at or above which the ticks stay at their tightest"
    default 350
    range 2 65535
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC && !KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE
    help
      Must be above COOLDOWN_SLOW_SPEED. See that option for the units.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_COOLDOWN_CURVE
    int "Shape of the climb between the two speeds"
    default 1
    range 1 4
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC && !KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE
    help
      1 closes the gap in a straight line, which already accelerates in
      ticks per second because the rate is the reciprocal of the gap.
      Higher values hold the gap near its widest through the lower part
      of the range and save the tightening for genuinely fast movement.

      This cannot make the ticks tighten *earlier* than a straight line.
      For that, lower COOLDOWN_FAST_SPEED instead.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK
    bool "Tick when a hold-tap settles on its hold"
    default n
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC && (!ZMK_SPLIT || ZMK_SPLIT_ROLE_CENTRAL)
    help
      A key bound to a hold-tap is not yet anything when it goes down: a
      letter if it is tapped, a modifier or a layer if it is held. The
      split between the two is invisible from the finger's side, so the
      only way to know is to count out the tapping term and hope. This
      returns a tick at that moment instead.

      Off, because it turned out to be unpleasant to type against. The
      pointer's tick answers a hand that is deliberately turning a ball;
      this one fires in the middle of typing, where the hand is already
      committed and has nothing to do with the information. Knowing the
      hold had landed was not worth being interrupted mid-word.

      Everything it needs is still here and still built -- the listener,
      the behaviour that carries a tick across the split link, its
      binding, and the node in the shield's dtsi. Setting this to y turns
      it back on with nothing else to put back.

      Only the central half can decide this, which is why the option is
      not offered on a peripheral: behaviours are resolved centrally, and
      the events this reads are not even built on the other side.

      The tick still lands on the half the key belongs to. The central
      remembers which side each held position came from and, for one on
      the other half, reaches its motor over the split link -- which
      carries behaviour invocations and nothing else, so the tick is
      dressed up as a behaviour to fit through. See
      src/behavior_haptic_tick.c.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK_MODS
    bool "Tick when a held key becomes a modifier"
    default y
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK_LAYERS
    bool "Tick when a held key turns a layer on"
    default y
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK_EFFECT
    int "Effect played when a hold settles"
    default 25
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK
    help
      25 is Sharp Tick 2 at 80%.

      This started at 6, Sharp Click at 30%, on the reasoning that a
      light tap should be light. It was too soft to read as a definite
      answer -- a hold either happened or it did not, and the tick has to
      say which. Sharp Tick is the same family as the pointer's own tick,
      one rung stronger than the 60% it uses, so the two are the same
      kind of sensation and the one that matters is the firmer one.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK_MIN_MS
    int "How long a key must have been down to count as held"
    default 40
    range 0 1000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_HOLD_TICK
    help
      Nothing distinguishes the modifier a hold-tap produces from a plain
      &kp LSHFT except that the key had been down for a while first, so
      without this every capital letter would tick.

      A plain binding fires within a millisecond of the key going down; a
      hold has waited out the tapping term, or at least until the next
      key arrived. 40 sits well clear of the first and well below the
      second.

      Set this to 0 to tick on every modifier and every layer, including
      ones that engage the moment the key goes down, such as &mo.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_COOLDOWN_IDLE_MS
    int "Gap that counts as the end of a scroll, in milliseconds"
    default 200
    range 20 2000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC && !KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_TICK_BY_DISTANCE
    help
      After this long without movement the speed is forgotten, so the
      next scroll starts from the widest spacing and builds again rather
      than resuming at the rate the last one ended on.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_DELAY_MS
    int "KobitoKey boot haptic delay in milliseconds"
    default 500
    range 0 10000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

# --- DRV2605L ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# The motor is now an LRA behind a DRV2605L rather than an ERM on a GPIO,
# so a buzz is "play effect N" instead of "hold the pin high for N ms".
# The chip ends the effect itself, which is why nothing here has to switch
# the motor back off.
#
# Effect numbers are the DRV2605 immersion library, with LIBRARY = 6 (LRA).
# A few useful ones: 1 strong click, 4 sharp click, 7 soft bump,
# 10 double click, 24 sharp tick 1, 47 buzz 1.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_EFFECT
    int "Effect played for pointer movement"
    default 24
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_EFFECT_LONG
    int "Effect played first at boot"
    default 50
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      50 is Buzz 4 at 40% -- the "buu" in buu-bu-bu. Toned down in steps
      from the original 47 (Buzz 1, 100%) through 49 (Buzz 3, 60%), which
      still felt strong against the two short clicks. 51 (Buzz 5, 20%) is
      the next step down if this is still too much.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_EFFECT_SHORT
    int "Effect played for the two short boot buzzes"
    default 2
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      2 is Strong Click at 60% -- the two "bu"s in buu-bu-bu. Same story
      as BOOT_EFFECT_LONG: toned down from the original 1 (100%).

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_GAP1_MS
    int "Gap between the long buzz and the first short one, in milliseconds"
    default 600
    range 0 1270
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Capped by the waveform sequencer's wait slot, which only encodes
      up to 1270ms (0x80-0xFF, 10ms steps).

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BOOT_GAP2_MS
    int "Gap between the two short buzzes, in milliseconds"
    default 150
    range 0 1270
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Same 1270ms ceiling as BOOT_GAP1_MS, for the same reason.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_USB_EFFECT
    int "Effect played when USB is connected"
    default 2
    range 1 123
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Same 60% strong click as the short boot buzz. See that option for
      why one step gets there.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_RATED_VOLTAGE
    int "DRV2605L RATED_VOLTAGE register value"
    default 109
    range 0 255
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Sets the steady-state drive level the closed loop aims for.

      The datasheet gives the RMS voltage produced by a given register
      value. An LRA is not driven for the whole period -- the driver stops
      to listen to the back-EMF -- so the RMS comes out below the drive
      amplitude by the square root of the fraction that is actually driven:

        Vrms = 20.71mV * RATED_VOLTAGE * sqrt(1 - 1.5ms * f_LRA)

      Going the other way, for the VLV041235L at 1.8 Vrms and 240 Hz:

        RATED_VOLTAGE = 1.8 / (0.02071 * 0.8) = 109

      This was 70 to begin with, from dividing where the equation
      multiplies. The motor ran at about two thirds of the voltage it is
      rated for, which is exactly what a weak buzz feels like.

      Raising it past 109 drives the motor harder than its rating.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_OD_CLAMP
    int "DRV2605L OD_CLAMP register value"
    default 175
    range 0 255
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Ceiling on the drive voltage while overdriving.

      This is not the steady-state level -- the loop regulates that to
      RATED_VOLTAGE -- it is how hard the driver may hit the motor to get
      it moving and to brake it again. That overdrive is where the crispness
      of an LRA comes from, so clamping it close to the rated level takes
      away the very thing the DRV2605L is for.

      This is the knob for making everything stronger. A library click
      lasts tens of milliseconds and barely reaches steady state, so most
      of what the hand feels is the overdrive, not the rated level.

      Set by ratio rather than from the datasheet equation: the constant
      for the LRA form of that equation could not be read out of the
      document with confidence. 147 was a third above RATED_VOLTAGE; 175
      is roughly another fifth on top, reached by feel rather than by
      calculation, which is worth knowing before trusting the number.

      Raising it does not drive the motor past its rating in steady state
      -- the loop still regulates to RATED_VOLTAGE -- it only lets the
      driver hit harder on the way there and while braking.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_BRAKE_FACTOR
    int "FB_BRAKE_FACTOR (0-7)"
    default 3
    range 0 7
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      0=1x 1=2x 2=3x 3=4x 4=6x 5=8x 6=16x 7=braking disabled.

      How hard the driver reverses to stop the mass. This is where the
      crispness of an LRA lives: the higher the factor, the shorter the
      ring-out.

      3 is 4x. 6 (16x) was tried and reverted -- not because it felt wrong
      but because it was changed at the same time as the loop gain and
      calibration started failing, so neither could be judged. Worth
      trying again on its own.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_LOOP_GAIN
    int "LOOP_GAIN (0-3)"
    default 1
    range 0 3
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      0=low 1=medium 2=high 3=very high. How aggressively the loop
      corrects towards the rated voltage.

      Medium, because it is what auto-calibration has actually succeeded
      under on this hardware. Low was tried, taken from a configuration
      that works for someone else with this motor, together with a 16x
      brake factor: calibration then failed on three boots out of three.
      That configuration assumes a 170 Hz actuator and this one does not
      behave like one, so its other numbers do not carry over either.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_DRIVE_TIME
    int "DRV2605L DRIVE_TIME field (CONTROL1 bits 4:0)"
    default 18
    range 0 31
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      How long the driver pushes before it stops to listen to the back-EMF.
      It has to line up with half the LRA's period, and the auto-calibration
      routine uses it as the starting point for finding resonance:

        drive time = 0.5ms + DRIVE_TIME * 0.1ms

      Measured rather than taken from the data sheet. The resonance
      register was read five times while the motor was being driven and
      returned period=46 every time, which is 4529us:

        DRIVE_TIME = (4529/2 - 500) / 100 = 18

      That is 220.79 Hz. The data sheet says 240 Hz and a configuration for
      this motor from elsewhere assumes 170 Hz; neither matches this build.
      Resonance moves with how the motor is mounted, so measuring beats
      copying.

      An earlier attempt read this register more than a second after
      playback had ended and got 236 Hz once and 423-508 Hz on other boots.
      The register is only meaningful while the loop is driving.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_AUTO_CAL_TIME
    int "DRV2605L AUTO_CAL_TIME (0-3)"
    default 3
    range 0 3
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      How long auto-calibration is allowed to drive the motor while it
      measures. 3 is the longest setting. Calibration happens once at boot,
      so there is little to gain by hurrying it and something to lose: a
      short window is one of the ways it fails to reach maximum back-EMF.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_AUTOCAL
    bool "Run DRV2605L auto-calibration at boot"
    default y
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Measures the motor and sets the compensation, back-EMF and gain
      registers. Takes well under a second and makes a short buzz of its
      own. The results are logged; once they are known and stable they can
      be written in directly and this turned off.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_CAL_COMP
    int "A_CAL_COMP written before any calibration"
    default 12
    range 0 255
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_CAL_BEMF
    int "A_CAL_BEMF written before any calibration"
    default 109
    range 0 255
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_CAL_BEMF_GAIN
    int "BEMF_GAIN (0-3) written before any calibration"
    default 2
    range 0 3
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      These three are the starting point every time the chip is brought
      up, and the only tuning the closed-lid USB buzz gets -- that path has
      no time for auto-calibration. Boot once with KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_AUTOCAL
      on, read the "Haptic calibrated" line out of the log, and copy the
      numbers here.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_PLAY_SETTLE_MS
    int "How long to wait for a boot-path buzz to finish"
    default 150
    range 10 1000
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Only used on the closed-lid USB boot, where the half powers off as
      soon as it has read the lid. The GO bit is polled and this is just
      the ceiling, so a short effect returns long before it.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_CAL_SURVEY
    int "Repeat calibration this many times at boot and log each result"
    default 0
    range 0 20
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Diagnostic. 0 disables it.

      One passing calibration is not evidence that the calibration was
      right: this part has returned DIAG_RESULT=0 while reporting a
      back-EMF of 22 and a resonance of 508 Hz, which is roughly twice the
      real figure. Numbers taken from a single run are worth what that run
      happened to lock onto.

      Running it repeatedly and printing every result shows whether the
      measurement is repeatable at all. Tight cluster: trust it. Scatter:
      this actuator cannot be characterised this way and the drive should
      be fixed rather than measured.

config KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC_WORKQ_STACK_SIZE
    int "Stack size for the haptic thread"
    default 768
    depends on KOBITOKEY_HAPTIC
    help
      Every register write happens on this thread, never on the input path
      and never on the system workqueue. A stuck bus then costs a buzz, not
      the pointer.

endmenu

